# Relux Proxy

Relux Proxy currently ships as a native macOS menu bar app that manages one SSH
dynamic SOCKS5 tunnel. It uses the system `/usr/bin/ssh`, so hosts, keys, jump
hosts, and other options from `~/.ssh/config` continue to work. The planned
versioned contract for saved, concurrent legacy SOCKS tunnels is
[`.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md`](.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md); it
is not shipped behavior yet.

The repository also contains the approved specifications and implementation
backlog for a future macOS/iOS system VPN over SSH. That VPN is not implemented
yet. Start with [the specification index](.spec/README.md),
[the current-state inventory](docs/current-state.md), and
[the saved task-board workflow](docs/project-management.md).

## Use

1. Open Relux Proxy from Applications.
2. Enter an SSH-reachable IP address, hostname, or `~/.ssh/config` alias, an
   optional account, and a local SOCKS port. Leave **Account** empty to let
   system OpenSSH use the matching config's `User` (or its normal default). A
   non-empty account is an explicit SSH user override.
3. Choose **Connect**.
4. In Firefox, open **Settings → Network Settings**, select **Manual proxy configuration**, and set:
   - SOCKS Host: `127.0.0.1`
   - Port: the port shown by Relux Proxy (`1080` by default)
   - SOCKS v5
   - Proxy DNS when using SOCKS v5

The initial defaults are `administrator@relux` and `127.0.0.1:1080`.

## Download

The latest signed and notarized build always has the same asset name and URL
(GitHub authentication is required while this repository is private):

https://github.com/relux-works/relux-proxy/releases/latest/download/ReluxProxy.dmg

Versioned DMGs and SHA-256 checksums are available on the GitHub Releases page.

## Development

Requirements: macOS 14 or newer and Xcode 15.3 or newer.

```bash
make test
make app
make run
```

### Development tools

| Command | Purpose | Output or effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `make test` | Run the SwiftPM test suite. | SwiftPM build and test data in `.build/`. |
| `make app` | Build and sign the macOS application. | `dist/ReluxProxy.app`. |
| `make dmg` | Build the app, then package a disk image. | `dist/ReluxProxy-v<version>-universal.dmg`. |
| `make run` | Build the app, then open it. | Opens `dist/ReluxProxy.app`. |

The app is intentionally not sandboxed because it needs to launch the system SSH executable. Release bundles use the hardened runtime and are signed with the Relux Works Developer ID.

Contributors must follow [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md). Planned VPN work is
tracked in `.task-board/`; no VPN implementation should begin without explicit
approval of the generated plan.

## Release

Push a semantic version tag to build, sign, notarize, staple, attest, and publish both versioned and stable-name DMGs:

```bash
git tag -s v0.1.0 -m "Relux Proxy v0.1.0"
git push origin v0.1.0
```

The release workflow expects `MACOS_CERT_P12`, `ASC_KEY_ID`, `ASC_ISSUER_ID`, and `ASC_KEY_P8` repository secrets. `ASC_KEY_P8` and `MACOS_CERT_P12` are base64 encoded.
# Current state and migration boundary

Last verified: 2026-07-28.

## What exists

The repository builds one SwiftPM macOS 14 executable, `ReluxProxy`, packaged by
shell scripts as a menu bar `.app` and DMG.

The app:

- stores one SSH host, account, and local SOCKS port in the legacy
  `AppStorage` keys `sshHost`, `sshAccount`, and `localPort`;
- has one shared `TunnelController` and can therefore run only one SSH child
  and one SOCKS tunnel at a time;
- launches `/usr/bin/ssh -N -C -D 127.0.0.1:<port>` with fail-fast, keepalive,
  timeout, batch-mode, and quiet logging options;
- passes an empty account as the bare host target, allowing system OpenSSH to
  use a matching `User` setting (or its normal default); a non-empty account is
  sent as `account@host`, which is an explicit SSH user override;
- accepts an IP address, hostname, or `Host` alias and lets the system client
  apply matching `~/.ssh/config` behavior such as `HostName`, `Port`,
  `IdentityFile`, and `ProxyJump`;
- binds the SOCKS listener only to IPv4 loopback (`127.0.0.1`) and keeps a
  trailing 4,000-character in-memory diagnostic for that one tunnel;
- reports connecting, connected, disconnecting, disconnected, and failed states;
- requires the user to configure a browser or other SOCKS-aware client manually;
- intentionally runs outside the App Sandbox so it can spawn system SSH.

Tests cover profile validation and exact SSH argument construction. CI runs
`swift test` and builds an ad-hoc-signed universal application bundle.

Tagged releases import the Relux Works Developer ID certificate, build a
universal app, sign the DMG, notarize and staple it, validate it with Gatekeeper,
publish checksums/provenance, and upload both versioned and stable
`ReluxProxy.dmg` assets.

## What does not exist

The current app is not a system VPN and does not contain:

- saved multi-profile configuration or concurrent SOCKS tunnels;
- iOS targets;
- `NETunnelProviderManager` or packet-tunnel extensions;
- a userspace TCP/IP stack or packetFlow bridge;
- an embedded SSH implementation;
- UDP or system DNS forwarding;
- the remote `relux-relay` executable/protocol;
- shared profile/Keychain state between host and extension;
- Network Extension entitlements or provisioning;
- TestFlight/App Store release automation.

## Migration boundary

[`../.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md`](../.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md)
is the versioned implementation contract for a planned extension of this legacy
macOS SOCKS mode. It defines the one-time migration of the three keys above,
profile persistence, OpenSSH account precedence, port ownership, and
independent multi-tunnel lifecycle. It does not claim that the current release
already has those capabilities.

The planned VPN product in `.spec/` remains a new architecture, not an
incremental toggle inside `TunnelController`. Existing source files remain the
current SOCKS product until board-approved migration tasks create the
multi-target project.

During migration:

- the stable macOS release path must remain reproducible;
- current host/account defaults and user expectations are preserved by the
  legacy multi-tunnel contract's one-time migration; any VPN-profile migration
  still needs its own explicit product decision;
- the system-SSH implementation may remain as a macOS legacy mode, be replaced,
  or be retired only through a product decision;
- SwiftPM tests and release history must not be silently discarded;
- Network Extension code must not be backported into the current manual app
  without the architecture and entitlement gates in `.spec/validation.md`.

